  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nRadar reflectivity has been quite active this morning, with \nconsistent returns across the area north of San Diego County. It's\nlikely the majority of that precipitation is not actually reaching\nthe ground with only an additional 0.01-0.08 inches of rain \nrecorded since 4 AM. Light rain is possible through the rest of \nthe day, with accumulations expected to be light and scattered in \nnature. An isolated thunderstorm or two cannot be ruled out, most \nlikely for the coast, valleys, and mountains north of San Diego \nCounty and in the High Desert. \n\nHigh cloud coverage continues to be widespread today, but is \nexpected to thin out tomorrow. Precipitable water values are \nexpected to remain elevated which will keep overnight lows elevated \nand conditions feeling tropical. The tropical feeling airmass is \nexpected to disrupt marine layer low cloud and fog development \nand may result in a weaker afternoon sea breeze. This will \ncontribute to high and low temperatures along the coast running 10\nto 15 degrees above average. Further inland, daytime highs will \nbe 5 to 10 degrees above average with a more notable increase in \nlow temperatures which are expectd to run 8 to 15 degrees above \naverage. Hottest conditions are expected Tuesday through Thursday \nfor the coast, valleys, and mountains below 5000 ft with hottest \nconditions expected in the low desert Wednesday through Friday. \nWidespread moderate to areas of major HeatRisk are expected. \n\nThe lingering moisture and increased sunshine for Tuesday and \nWednesday will increase chances of afternoon showers and \nthunderstorms over the mountains. There is expected to be little to \nno additional forcing outside of the usual diurnal heating so \ncoverage of any thunderstorms should be fairly isolated. By \nThursday, a transient ridge of high pressure is expected to move \ndirectly over Southern California. This would result in an airmass \nlikely too stable for thunderstorm development, although with \nlingering moisture in the air a few showers cannot be ruled out over \nthe mountains. \n\nThere is a spread in potential outcomes for the upper level pattern \nover the upcoming weekend. 32 percent of ensemble solutions have a \ntrough over the Pacific Northwest, which would bring noticeable \nrelief from the heat and humidity. 17 percent of solutions keep the \nridge overhead which would prolong the heat event. The rest of the \nsolutions are somewhere in between, with a slower passage of the \ntrough but a weakening of the ridge. Current forecast follows the \nNBM and is more inline with the \"in between\" solutions with a \ngradual decrease in both high and low temperatures Friday through \nSunday.",
